<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Delft</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Delft is a city in the province of South Holland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, which received town rights in 1246. It prospered from brewing, cloth and later the blue-and-white Delftware pottery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a> was assassinated in the Prinsenhof there in 1584 and lies in its New Church, the burial place of the House of Orange, and the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-vermeer">Johannes Vermeer</a> worked in the city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/delft.jpg" alt="Delft" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zairon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delft_Blick_von_der_Nieuwe_Kerk_auf_die_Oude_Kerk_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rebels offered sovereignty first to Francis, Duke of Anjou, and, after William the Silent was assassinated at <b>Delft</b> in 1584, to Elizabeth I of England, who declined it but sent troops under the Earl of Leicester.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Hooke’s Micrographia (1665) and the letters of Antonie van Leeuwenhoek from <b>Delft</b> opened the world of the microscope; Christiaan Huygens designed the first pendulum clock and explained the ring of Saturn; Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz developed the calculus independently of Newton and published it first, in 1684.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Catholics tended to keep to their own section of each town: the painter Johannes Vermeer lived in the “Papist corner” of <b>Delft</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Balthasar Gérard shot William of Orange, leader of the Dutch revolt against Spain, at his home in <b>Delft</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-10">10 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts drained peat bogs, built dykes and founded towns such as Haarlem, <b>Delft</b>, Leiden and Dordrecht.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-holland">County of Holland</a></li></ul></section>
